|> Exercise
    handle=_gas_tank_decimal_approximation_
    Give a decimal approximation to
    $$
    r(1 + \eta/2)
    $$
    for $r = 100$m, accurate within $\pm 5\%$. (No calculators.)

    |> Solution

        Since

        $$
        \eta \approx {11\over 7}
        $$

        within 1/1000th, we have

        $$
        \begin{align*}
        \   r(1 + \eta/2)\,\, \approx&\,\,\,\dn{1.3}\up{0.0} r\left(1 + {11\over 14}\right)\\
        \                           =&\,\,\,\dn{1.3}\up{1.3} r\left({14 + 11\over 14}\right)\\
        \                           =&\,\,\,\dn{1.3}\up{1.3} r\cdot {25\over 14}\\
        \                           =&\,\,\,\dn{1.3}\up{1.3} r\cdot {28 - 3\over 14}\\
        \                           =&\,\,\,\dn{1.3}\up{1.3} r\left(2 - {3\over 14}\right)\\
        \                     \approx&\,\,\,\dn{1.3}\up{1.3} r\left(2 - {3\over 15}\right)\\
        \                           =&\,\,\,\dn{1.3}\up{1.3} r\left(2 - {1\over 5}\right)\\
        \                           =&\,\,\,\dn{1.3}\up{1.3} r(2 - 0.2)\\
        \                           =&\,\,\,\dn{0.0}\up{1.3} 1.8r
        \end{align*}
        $$

        leading to an approximation of

        $$
        \approx 180\te{m}
        $$
        for $r = 100$m.
        For a different, coarser approach, one can
        even use
        $$
        \eta \approx {10.5\over 7} = 1.5
        $$
        which gives
        $$
        1 + \eta/2 \approx 1.75
        $$
        and a final approximation of
        $$
        \approx 175\te{m}
        $$
        for $r = 100$m.

        |> SolutionNote

            The two approximations given produce
            1$\%$ and 2$\%$ of error, respectively, 
            or $\approx 0.8\%$ and $\approx 1.9\%$, 
            more exactly, respectively. (For the
            second approximation,

            $$
            1.5
            $$

            is nearly 5$\%$ less than $\eta/2$ right
            off the bat, but the fraction represented
            by this error is diluted by the fact that
            we add 1.)

        |> SolutionNote

            The true value is $178.5$m, as 

            $$
            \eta/2 = 0.785\dots
            $$

            and some, as

            $$
            \eta = 1.570\dots
            $$

            and some. (Note that half of $0.07$ is $0.035$,
            and that $0.75 + 0.035 = 0.785$.) (PS: In fact,
            doing the division

            $$
            1.57/2
            $$

            “by head” constitutes another perfectly valid
            method of approximation, if you can remember
            that $\eta = 1.570\dots$.)
